# untested -- programs that had neither a card that ran them nor a case,
# worked out on 2026-09-01/02.
#
# THE FIRST SWEEP GOT FOUR OF THEM WRONG BY TYPING THE WRONG PATH.
# `bincheckr', `mkdict' and `mkindex' are in CMDS/GAMES and `byteflip' is in
# CMDS/NEWS; run as `/dd/CMDS/<name>' all four answer E$PNNF, and it reads
# exactly like a program that cannot find its data.  Nothing was wrong with
# any of them.  That is the same mistake this collection keeps making and it
# is recorded here rather than quietly fixed.
#
# Not assertable here, and why: `config' prints a genuine table of this
# machine's C type properties and then takes an ILLEGAL instruction in its
# floating-point section; `bincheckr' reads the book header and then faults
# at close; `crypto' as a FILTER ends the session even with its output
# redirected.  datatest fails any case whose session took an exception, which
# is right, so those three live on gallery cards instead.

family  untested

setup   export TERM=vt100
setup   export TERMCAP=/dd/SYS/termcap
setup   W=/dd/tmp/UTC
setup   /dd/CMDS/rm -f $W/* > /nil 2>/nil
setup   /dd/CMDS/mkdir -p $W > /nil 2>/nil

case    yagi-designs-a-twelve-element-two-metre-beam
# It READS FROM STANDARD INPUT and asks FIVE questions: centre frequency,
# element count, boom diameter, whether the elements are insulated from the
# boom, and a tubing size off its own list.  Given four answers it loops on
# the fifth forever -- EOF on a numeric read returns the same thing every
# time -- which is how it came to be recorded as silent.
run     echo "144" > $W/y.in
run     echo "12" >> $W/y.in
run     echo "3" >> $W/y.in
run     echo "N" >> $W/y.in
run     echo "3" >> $W/y.in
run     /dd/CMDS/yagi < $W/y.in
expect  This program designs UHF/VHF yagi antennas based on designs by DL6WU.
expect  Frequency: 144.00 MHz
expect  Wavelength: 2.082m
expect  # of elements: 12
expect  Maximum practical gain: 12.8 dBD
expect  Element tubing diameter: 8.7mm
expect  reflector      1017.2                  0.0
expect  director #6     875.5               3206.1

case    crypto-is-a-cryptogram-solver-with-a-real-option-list
# Its bare answer is two lines of usage naming -h and -i; -h is the rest.
run     /dd/CMDS/crypto -h
expect  This program allows you to interactively solve cryptogram puzzles
expect  -e	Encode the input stream with a random cypher.
expect  -cSTR	encode using the Code STR as the encryption cypher.
expect  -i	output the Interactive commands.
expect  fortune -l | crypto -r

case    helpindex-says-what-it-cannot-index
# `helpindex' bare says `Missing names of files to create index for.'  Given
# files it does not want, it names them -- which is more than most programs
# here manage.
run     /dd/CMDS/helpindex
run     /dd/CMDS/helpindex /dd/DOC/README-KSH /dd/DOC/README-CIO
expect  helpindex: Missing names of files to create index for.
expect  ** No index created for file "/dd/DOC/README-KSH".
expect  ** No index created for file "/dd/DOC/README-CIO".

case    the-four-that-are-not-in-CMDS
# The correction, asserted so it cannot be made a third time: at the wrong
# path they answer E$PNNF, at the right one they are there.  `byteflip' is
# only LISTED and not run -- running it ends the emulator session, which the
# newshist card records.
run     /dd/CMDS/bincheckr
run     /dd/CMDS/mkdict
run     /dd/CMDS/ls /dd/CMDS/GAMES/bincheckr /dd/CMDS/GAMES/mkdict /dd/CMDS/GAMES/mkindex /dd/CMDS/NEWS/byteflip
expect  /dd/CMDS/bincheckr:  (E$PNNF)
expect  /dd/CMDS/mkdict:  (E$PNNF)
expect  /dd/CMDS/GAMES/bincheckr
expect  /dd/CMDS/GAMES/mkdict
expect  /dd/CMDS/GAMES/mkindex
expect  /dd/CMDS/NEWS/byteflip
